<!-- sdt:begin:instructions/delegation -->

Read this when you manage a delegate through `sdt agent task`. For a corpus
task claim or a handoff between sessions, see `tasks-handoff.md`.

## Dispatch

Check the project's `.sdt.yaml` for `delegation.agents`, `max_attempts` and
`max_duration`. Choose a role and the capabilities the task requires. Give
the delegate one bounded goal, its allowed scope, and acceptance criteria.
The principal owns task decisions, integration and closeout.

Record the returned runtime ID alongside the plan item or corpus task. That
ID follows the worker, its artifacts and the retained worktree. A
`context task claim` records ownership of a corpus task and does not launch a
worker.

Use `--reference` to copy project files as stable context, `--commit` to
select a source revision and `--include` to carry explicitly selected dirty
paths. For an existing worktree, pair `--worktree` with the matching `--id`.
The checkout must belong to the project repository, be clean, and be at the
source commit on branch `sdt/delegation/<id>` under
`.sdt/delegation/worktrees/<id>`. Use `--include` only with a new worktree.
If Git cannot update repository metadata, report that failure before retrying.
A worktree separates Git edits. The worker's tools still run with the
permissions of their environment.

## Observe and decide

| Observation | Next action |
|---|---|
| Queued or running | Use `sdt agent task wait <id> --timeout 2m`. If it times out, inspect `status <id>`. The timeout ends the wait only. |
| Worker health is unknown | Keep the recorded task state. Verify process visibility before trying to recover or start a replacement. |
| Permission pending | Use `permissions list <id>`. Inspect the request and offered options. Resolve only within authority already granted, otherwise ask the user. Use `permissions resolve <id> <request-id> --option <option-id> --rationale "<reason>"` with the exact offered ID. |
| Result ready | Read `result <id>` and relevant artifacts with `result <id> <artifact>`. Verify what changed and the evidence against the brief. Use `accept <id>` when satisfied, or `correct <id> --instructions "<required change>"` for bounded follow-up work. |
| Intervention required without a pending permission | Inspect recent events in `status <id>` and retrieve the worker log with `result <id> --log`. Address the cause and check remaining limits before `resume <id>`. |
| Provider quota or availability restriction | Use `availability list` to see exclusions and expiry. Respect the provider retry time or local cooldown. A local attempt limit does not measure the remaining provider quota. Reset a key only when authorized and there is evidence the restriction has cleared. |
| Work should stop | Use `cancel <id>` when authorized. Confirm the resulting state before taking further action. |
| Accepted | Review `apply preview <id>` and apply the reviewed digest when integration is authorized. |
| Completed, failed or cancelled | Inspect retained artifacts and changes before deciding whether to request cleanup. |

Commands in the table use the `sdt agent task` prefix. Use
`sdt schema 'agent task <command>'` to confirm flags.

## Review and integrate

For example, dispatch a narrowly scoped correction:

```sh
sdt agent task start --role backend --capability file-write --brief "Fix the typo in README.md. Change only that line and report the diff."
sdt agent task wait <id> --timeout 2m
sdt agent task result <id>
```

Use the returned ID in place of `<id>`. After independently verifying the
result, accept it and preview the patch:

```sh
sdt agent task accept <id>
sdt agent task apply preview <id>
```

Review the paths, patch and conflicts. If integration is authorized, run
`sdt agent task apply execute <id> --digest <reviewed-digest>`. If the digest
changes, review a fresh preview. Application writes to the principal working
tree. Staging and committing are separate actions.

## Retire

Use `sdt agent task cleanup preview <id>` to inspect retained resources. Make
sure the principal has what it needs from the worktree and artifacts. Get
explicit authorization before running `sdt agent task cleanup execute <id>`.

